<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ramesses III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">assassinated c. 1153 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ramesses III was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twentieth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twentieth Dynasty</a> of the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>. He defeated land and sea invasions by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a> around 1177 BCE, preserving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> during the Late Bronze Age collapse. He was assassinated in a harem conspiracy around 1153 BCE, after which the New Kingdom declined under a series of weak successors.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramesses-iii.jpg" alt="Ramesses III" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Asavaa · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KhonsuTemple-Karnak-RamessesIII-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ramesses III was the son of Setnakhte and Tiy-Merenese, and was not closely related to Ramesses I or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a>. Setnakhte's origin is unknown. He seized the throne during a time of crisis and unrest, probably from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tausret">Twosret</a>, and was presumably a minor descendant of Ramesses II through a different family line from that of Seti II, Siptah and Twosret. Scholars differ on the dates of Ramesses III's reign, and one calculation gives about 31 years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His reign fell in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age-collapse">Late Bronze Age collapse</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> faced invaders including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyans">Libyans</a>, along with economic difficulties. In Year 5 and Year 11 he fought Libyan tribesmen in the Western Delta. In Year 8 the Sea Peoples, among them the Peleset, Denyen, Shardana and Tjekker, invaded by land and sea. He defeated them at the Battle of Djahy on the eastern frontier and at the Battle of the Delta, where he ambushed their ships at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> with a fleet and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> on the shore. He settled the defeated Sea Peoples in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, and their presence there may have contributed to the rise of states such as Philistia.</p><p>The cost of these wars drained the treasury. In Year 29 the first known labour strike in recorded history took place at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deir-el-medina">Deir el-Medina</a>, when rations could not be supplied to the royal tomb-builders. His monuments ignore such troubles. He added to the temples at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medinet-habu">Medinet Habu</a>, one of the largest and best-preserved funerary temples in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, enclosed by massive fortifications.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Trial papyri record a harem conspiracy against his life, made at a celebration at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medinet-habu">Medinet Habu</a>. It was instigated by his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiye">Tiye</a>, who hoped her son Pentaweret would inherit; Ramesses III had not clearly settled the succession among his sons. Many officials were implicated, three trials were held, and 38 people were sentenced to death. A CT scan of his mummy showed a deep knife wound across the throat, which would have been rapidly fatal.</p><p>The conspirators failed in one respect: the crown passed to his designated successor, Ramesses IV. The succession crisis accelerated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s decline, and Ramesses III is considered the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> to have wielded substantial power.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ramesses III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Bronze Age: the list of the pharaoh Seti I from c. 1215 BCE, the chronicle of a campaign by <b>Ramesses III</b>, and a letter from a scribe at a border fortress in the Wadi Tumilat under Merneptah reporting &quot;shasu-tribes of Edom&quot; moving to watering holes in Egyptian territory.</blockquote><a href="/empire/edom">Edom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…obligations on a scale that eventually made the Amun priesthood a rival power to the crown; by the reign of <b>Ramesses III</b>, according to the Great Harris Papyrus, the temples of Amun held roughly two-thirds of all temple lands in Egypt and some ninety percent of temple-owned ships.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>At the end of the period, Egyptian inscriptions of Merneptah and <b>Ramesses III</b> list among the Sea Peoples groups such as the Ekwesh and the Denyen, whom some scholars connect with the Achaeans and the Danaans.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mycenaean-civilisation">Mycenaean civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Egypt survived, diminished, after <b>Ramesses III</b> fought off the invaders whom modern historians call the Sea Peoples.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-collapse-problem">The Collapse Problem</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 29th year of <b>Ramesses III</b> (about 1157 BCE), when their rations were weeks late, they downed tools in the first recorded strike.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>References fade after the New Kingdom, with the last expeditions recorded under <b>Ramesses III</b> in the 12th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/land-of-punt">Land of Punt</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Thebes, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=25.72,32.61&amp;year=-1153" aria-label="Thebes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5413 1218 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ramesses III"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5863 1488h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5863 1488h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=25.72,32.61&amp;year=-1153">What was here in 1153 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I caused the whole country to be planted with groves of trees and with flowering shrubs, and I made the people to sit under the shade thereof.&#8221;</p><footer>Great Harris Papyrus, summary of his reign &#183; tr. E. A. Wallis Budge, 1914 &#183; the account of his reign written in his name in the Great Harris Papyrus, completed after his death &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15932/pg15932-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I made it possible for an Egyptian woman to walk with a bold step to the place whither she wished to go; no strange man attacked her, and no one on the road.&#8221;</p><footer>Great Harris Papyrus, summary of his reign &#183; tr. E. A. Wallis Budge, 1914 &#183; the account of his reign written in his name in the Great Harris Papyrus, completed after his death &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15932/pg15932-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Their bows and their weapons of war lay idle in their barracks, and they ate their fill and drank their fill with shouts of joy.&#8221;</p><footer>Great Harris Papyrus, summary of his reign &#183; tr. E. A. Wallis Budge, 1914 &#183; the account of his reign written in his name in the Great Harris Papyrus, completed after his death; of his soldiers in peacetime &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15932/pg15932-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
